 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Dominated by the vision-language model feature extraction (offline) and the contrastive loss computation which involves pairwise similarities. The contrastive mechanism adds overhead compared to simple L1 semantic loss [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing 512-dimensional semantic embeddings for each Gaussian primitive, increasing memory usage compared to color-only representations [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Prevents optimization instability from noisy distilled features; ensures discriminative semantic boundaries; eliminates boundary bleeding in semantic fields [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Sensitive to the quality of distilled feature maps; requires careful balancing of loss weights to prevent degradation of geometric quality [S1].
-- **When to use:** When training 3D Gaussian Splatting models with semantic or vision-language embeddings where noise in distilled features is a concern [S1].
+*   **Complexity**: Adds overhead for feature map generation and contrastive loss computation. The contrastive loss requires identifying negative samples, increasing per-step computation compared to simple L1 semantic loss [S1].
+*   **Strengths**: Prevents optimization instability caused by noise in distilled feature maps by using relative similarity (contrastive) rather than absolute distance (MSE) [S1]. Eliminates boundary bleeding issues in semantic fields by leveraging photometric loss for spatial accuracy [S1].
+*   **Limitations**: Requires pre-computed dense feature maps from a vision-language model, adding a preprocessing step [S1]. Sensitive to the choice of negative sampling strategy for the contrastive loss [S1].
+*   **When to use**: When open-world semantic understanding is required alongside geometric reconstruction [S1].
+*   **When not to use**: When only photometric accuracy is needed, or when computational resources for feature distillation are unavailable [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the context of open-world 3D vision-language scene representation using Gaussian Splatting [S1]. The method addresses the limitation of global image embeddings from foundational vision-language models by employing multi-scale feature distillation for localized semantic granularity [S1]. It builds on the standard Gaussian Splatting optimization loop but replaces simple regression losses with contrastive alignment for semantic fields [S1].
+Originated in the paper "Open-World 3D Vision-Language Scene Representation with Gaussian Splatting and Cross-Modal Alignment" [S1]. It addresses the limitation of global image embeddings from vision-language models by using multi-scale feature distillation for localized semantic granularity [S1]. It replaces simple MSE for semantic loss with contrastive loss to handle noise in distilled features [S1].
 
